{%- comment -%}
  What a listing's photographs actually show — and how many of them are really this listing's.

      {%- capture photos -%}{%- render 'photo-source', product: product -%}{%- endcapture -%}
      {%- capture n -%}{%- render 'photo-source', product: product, style: 'count' -%}{%- endcapture -%}

  style: 'kind' (default) — one word:
    exact  the part itself was photographed
    donor  the pictures are of the donor vehicle the part came off, not of the part
    none   the listing has no usable photograph — nothing, or a single stray frame that
           belongs to neither this part nor a donor shoot

  style: 'count' — an integer: how many of product.media share the featured image's
    filename stem, i.e. come from the same shoot as the photo every surface vouches for.
    Some listings have picked up stray images of other parts entirely — a screen listed
    with alternator and engine photos. Those are not this listing's; they must not be
    counted on a card or shown in the gallery, and the gallery/JSON-LD only ever trust
    the featured image, which is checked here.

  CoreYard names every real part photo <R#>_NN.jpg, where R# is the SKU; a donor photo is
  named <donor-key>_NN.jpg instead, so its filename stem is not the SKU. That catches
  legacy listings predating CoreYard's donor-photo note as well as new ones (checked
  against every listing carrying that note — the stem never matched the SKU — and against
  parts photographed individually, where it always did).

  A stem that is neither the SKU used to be trusted as a donor photo outright. It is not
  enough: CoreYard keys donor photos by the source system's vehicle id, whose number space
  overlaps R#, and a mis-shared frame from another part lands under the same <digits>_NN.jpg
  name. A real donor shoot always attaches several frames (six by default), so 'donor' now
  also needs the featured stem to appear on more than one of the listing's images; a lone
  odd frame is 'none' — a placeholder the shopper can read, not a mirror or a visor
  presented as the donor car. Fixing the association itself is CoreYard's job; this just
  stops the storefront vouching for what it cannot identify.

  This lived inline in main-product.liquid, where only the product page could reach it, so
  a card, a search result and the Product structured data all went on claiming the photo
  was the part. Anything that shows a listing's photograph asks here first.
{%- endcomment -%}
{%- liquid
  assign style = style | default: 'kind'

  assign featured_stem = ''
  if product.featured_media
    assign featured_name = product.featured_media | image_url: width: 1 | split: '?' | first | split: '/' | last
    assign featured_stem = featured_name | split: '_' | first
  endif

  comment
    How many of the listing's media carry the featured image's stem — a real shoot attaches
    several frames, a stray reused frame stands alone.
  endcomment
  assign own = 0
  if featured_stem != blank
    for media in product.media
      assign media_name = media | image_url: width: 1 | split: '?' | first | split: '/' | last
      assign media_stem = media_name | split: '_' | first
      if media_stem == featured_stem
        assign own = own | plus: 1
      endif
    endfor
  endif

  if style == 'count'
    echo own
  else
    assign source = 'none'
    if product.featured_media
      assign source = 'exact'
      comment
        One variant per part, so the first variant's SKU is the part's R#. Fall back through
        selected_or_first_available_variant because a card renders no variant of its own.
      endcomment
      assign sku = product.selected_or_first_available_variant.sku | default: product.variants.first.sku
      if sku != blank and featured_stem != blank and featured_stem != sku
        if own > 1
          assign source = 'donor'
        else
          assign source = 'none'
        endif
      endif
    endif
    echo source
  endif
-%}
